        void Guide()
        {
            Rect(0,0,1600,900,ink);Text(65,35,1400,66,"The Wildfront handbook",title,49);Text(68,112,1400,40,"Plan your rotation. Read your equipment. Respect the wildlife—especially when the sky turns violet.",body,20);
            string[] tabs={"Match & storm","Guns & attachments","Wildlife & biomes","Travel & survival","Teams & controls","Locations","Building & editing"};for(int i=0;i<tabs.Length;i++)if(Button(65,204+i*88,315,65,tabs[i],guideTab==i)){guideTab=i;guideScroll=Vector2.zero;}
            string[] heads={"Choose freely. Stay ahead of the storm.","Every attachment has a specific job.","The storm changes the food chain.","Mobility is worth maintaining.","Share a plan, share your supplies.","Forty-eight places to build your route.","Harvest. Build cover. Edit a route."};
            string[] copy={
                "Solo, Duos, or Squads support eight participants. Practice opponents fill empty slots. Everyone marks ready before the host starts the draft.\n\nSpend ten tokens between a starting kit and selection priority. Click anywhere inside the map to preview a point; confirm during your selection turn. Nearby obstructions may shift the landing a few metres. Water spawns begin swimming. Teammates choose independently and all draft decisions are public. Earlier picks reserve a 60-metre area against opposing landings; teammates may regroup inside it.\n\nForty-eight settlements and hamlets connect through seventy-one road links. Sealed field caches in the smaller hamlets hold an attachment, medicine and armor plates. Field orders repeat after completion.\n\nSeven storm cycles alternate waiting and closing. The current ring is violet; the next safe zone is gold. Damage rises from 2 to 14 health each second outside the ring and bypasses armor. Closing periods enrage all wildlife. Supply flyovers deliver contestable high-tier crates. Each claimed crate also has a 35% motorcycle bonus chance, 25% survey-beacon chance, or 40% ordinary reward outcome. A motorcycle needs safe dry space nearby; an aquatic or blocked delivery substitutes a beacon.\n\nThe last surviving team wins. Late arrivals spectate until the next draft. A complete day and night passes every five minutes while in a reserve.",
                "Ridgeline: bolt-action hunting rifle. Trail Scout: versatile carbine. Brushmaster: close-range shotgun. Service Nine: sidearm. Kestrel: fast SMG. Longwatch: scoped precision rifle. Sawmill: sustained automatic fire. Eight classes use five ammunition families. Stormbreak RPG fires a traveling rocket with blast falloff and cover protection. Pistols are semi-auto; shotguns use pellets and pump timing; SMGs and assault/support guns are automatic; marksman rifles are semi-auto and snipers are bolt-action.\n\nThree weapon slots accept an optic, a barrel attachment, and a handling attachment. Find mods in settlements, select a gun in Inventory, then choose a compatible attachment from your pack. Replaced mods return to your pack. Attachments travel with a dropped gun.\n\nReflex / Ranger / Marksman optics give 1.5× / 3× / 6× aim. The 6× optic fits rifles and the support gun. Reinforced barrel: +12% damage. Precision barrel: 25% tighter spread. Muzzle brake: 30% less recoil. Speed loader: 20% faster reload. Steady grip: 20% tighter spread and 15% less recoil. Balanced stock: 25% less recoil.\n\nRarity improves base weapon damage. Crouching, aiming, movement and sustained fire also affect accuracy. Range damage falloff is stronger on SMGs and shotguns than rifles. There is no hidden materials-based gun tuning.",
                "Lush valley: deer, stags, hares, boar and elk among fields and forest. Dune basin: foxes, hares, pronghorn and horses. Ice desert: wolves, arctic foxes, snowshoe hares and elk. High Rockies: ibex, alpacas, stags, wolves and hares. Rainforest: forest boar, deer, wild oxen, hares and foxes. Reedwater marsh: boar, deer, hares, foxes and wild oxen near the shoreline.\n\nWolves and boar are naturally hostile. Deer, rabbits and other non-hostile wildlife flee during ordinary conditions. While the storm closes, every animal gains a violet tint and attacks nearby players. Their chance of dropping restorative rich meat rises from 8% to 65%.\n\nAnimals drop food and occasionally rich meat, which restores 20 health and 15 hunger on collection. They do not drop guns, ammunition, fuel or manufactured attachments.\n\nSharks patrol five lakes. They hunt swimmers, and cannot attack players seated in boats. Birds are ambient flocks. Nearby boar, canids, ibex and elk have spatial calls; smaller prey has quiet rustling. Wildlife is denser and small herds replenish near players outside their forward view. Subtle paw markers identify visible animals within 95 metres without showing them through cover. Several land species share adapted models.",
                "Estate wagons, dune buggies, service pickups, utility vans, trail motorcycles and motor launches have different top speeds and seating. WASD drives and steers; mouse-look is independent. Reverse steering follows the direction of travel. Brake before tight turns. Boost recharges but consumes more fuel.\n\nEngine condition reduces speed. Repair costs two materials; refuelling uses one can; a vehicle upgrade costs three materials. Some engines begin dead. Repair lay-bys along the roads have extra transport, fuel, parts and a sealed cache. Additional launches wait on every lake and along the river. Boats need sufficiently deep water; stepping out begins swimming. Boat occupants and car passengers can fire. Use bridges for road crossings.\n\nThe HUD separates health, armor, a ten-segment hunger meter and stamina, with icons, values and a food key reminder. Early warnings begin below 40 hunger. Hunger drains faster when running and more slowly while riding. Below 20 you cannot sprint; below 10 you slow down; at zero you take starvation damage. Food restores 35 hunger and 5 health. Medkits restore 45 health.\n\nLight / Field / Heavy vests absorb 25% / 35% / 50% of bullet and animal damage while durability remains. Storm and hunger bypass them. Plates restore 30 durability. Inventory drops can be shared and float on water.",
                "The lobby supports ready checks and team selection. In Duos and Squads, an active teammate allows downing before elimination. Hold Interact within three metres for three seconds to revive. Taking damage interrupts the rescue; bleed-out lasts 30 seconds.\n\nThe hunger meter shows ten food segments, an explicit state and the Eat key. Sprinting locks below 20; starvation harms health.\n\nThe map shows teammates, names, nearby living animals within 160 metres, supply drops and both storm rings. Enemy positions disappear after deployment. A supply-crate survey beacon (Q) briefly reveals enemy snapshots to its team for 12 seconds and warns everyone. Carry at most two; they can be dropped and shared. Live vehicle icons update once per second; enemy-occupied vehicles stay hidden. Scroll zooms and dragging pans. Site names grow clearer as you zoom; hovering a marker shows its details.\n\nVoice is disabled initially. Enable push-to-talk or open mic in Settings. All nearby players, including rivals, hear one another within 75 metres with distance attenuation. Incoming speech uses a jitter buffer and burst-aware packet handling. Headphones reduce feedback.\n\nAfter elimination, Solo players can spectate any survivor. In Duos/Squads, spectating stays within your team while teammates survive; once the team is eliminated, cycle through all survivors with LMB/RMB or arrow keys.\n\nRebind gameplay keys and mouse buttons in Settings → Key bindings. Escape remains available for the menu. A flashlight helps during the five-minute day/night cycle. Internet guests need the host address and a reachable UDP port; nearby sessions can be discovered on the local network."
            };
            System.Array.Resize(ref copy,7);copy[6]="Spawn with 80 wood, 40 stone and 20 metal. Select the pickaxe with ` and swing with LMB. Trees yield wood, rocks stone, and metallic ore yields metal. Supply stacks can also be found, dropped and shared. Each resource has HP and disappears consistently for all players when depleted.\n\nZ toggles building. F1 wall, F2 floor, F3 stairs, F4 pyramid. LMB places or holds to build; RMB changes material and R rotates. Each piece costs 10 materials. Wood/stone/metal have 300/450/600 maximum HP and build up gradually. Attach pieces to ground, static structures or existing builds. Destroying supports collapses unsupported sections. Each player may have 80 pieces, within a 512-piece world budget.\n\nAim at your team's piece and press H. Paint cells directly on the world grid or in the side panel. H confirms, RMB resets, R rotates stairs/pyramids and Escape cancels. Walls support windows, doors, low walls and diagonal shapes. E opens/closes an edited door. G repairs a nearby allied piece for two materials per 50 HP. Enemies cannot edit your team's structures.\n\nSprint is faster with less fatigue. Press crouch while running to slide; momentum, collision and visible stance replicate to other players. Settings do not pause gravity or the shared world.\n\nDuring a closing storm, nearby enraged land animals have a bounded chance to mutate into larger, tougher bosses. Boss rewards remain food and restorative meat. Players only show hostile HP after confirmed damage, briefly and without seeing through cover.";
            if(guideTab==5){var list=new System.Text.StringBuilder();foreach(var site in ExpeditionLayout.Regions)list.AppendLine(site.name+" · "+ReserveBiomes.Names[site.biome]+"\n"+site.description+"\n");System.Array.Resize(ref copy,6);copy[5]=list.ToString();}
            Text(435,205,1090,60,heads[guideTab],type,29,paper);guideScroll=GUI.BeginScrollView(new Rect(430,281,1100,510),guideScroll,new Rect(0,0,1065,Mathf.Max(850,copy[guideTab].Length/70f*28+500)));Text(8,5,1040,Mathf.Max(830,copy[guideTab].Length/70f*28+480),copy[guideTab],body,21,paper);GUI.EndScrollView();if(Button(1130,815,375,52,"Back",true))Back();
        }
